
# ===== CHAPTER 3: (title at the chapter's end)
# ============================== YEAR 5 ==============================
R(5, "Drought", "blight")
act("Reina turns the year's I Ching card: Drought. Every province collects an average harvest; no disasters. The population holds at x1, 100 million.")
say("Reina", "Drought. \"Not a cloud in months. The locusts, at least, are having a fine year.\" Every province collects an average harvest. The population holds at one hundred million, times one, so everyone reads the first row of their table. No disasters are named, so nobody turns a page in their booklet. An ordinary year. I'll remind you that the crown has kept its word in ordinary years before.",
 "Reina: An average year means I can't call it fat, and I've taxed fifty four times running. This is the year I put a canal section on the coast before Silke and Paddy find a third friend to sign their revolt.")
harvest("Rich, the Gentry, pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile.")
say("Rich", "Paid out to the grain, friends: fifteen to Her Majesty, thirty-five to Clark, Silke and Paddy, fifty-five to the Gentry's humble granary, and ten to Rob. And the rent stays the customary five, a dry year being no excuse for a thirsty landlord. Paddy, I hope that buys the Gentry a little goodwill on the coast. Her Majesty has kept her word in ordinary years, and so has the Gentry.",
 "Rich: Rob's soldier is one round from my Bowl and Paddy's vote is for sale, so this is the year to keep both peasants fond of me and not squeeze them. Five grain each costs me nothing next to having friends on the day someone calls a revolt.")
rents(5, "Rents: Rich asks Silke and Paddy for the minimum rent, and each pays him 5.")
say("Reina", "Fifty again: the fifth year running, and you can set your calendars by it. In return the crown digs the first section of the Grand Canal through Province 6, at the crown's own cost. Paddy, you gave me a year out loud. Here's what I give you for it, and it'll still be paying you ten a harvest long after this drought is forgotten. Clark keeps his fifteen.",
 "Reina: I won't cut the rate, but a canal on the coast is a monument too, and it takes away Paddy's reason to sign the next revolt. Silke can watch the water run past her border and ask herself who her friends are.")
act("Reina declares a tax of 50% for the fifth year running: 17.5 each from Clark, Silke and Paddy and 27.5 from Rich, 80 in all, and 5 from Rob, who has always refused his. The crown will dig the first section of the Grand Canal in Paddy's Province 6, all 40 from the reserve (+10 to Province 6's harvest from next year), and pay Clark 15 at the year's end.", map_hl=[1,6])
act("Clark, Rich, Silke, Paddy and Rob are each asked at the same time, before the Official collects: pay the tax, or call a revolt and name the cause, or say they'd back one.", map_hl=[2,3,5,6,8])
say("Clark", "Seventeen and a half, paid, counted twice and written down once. Five years at fifty percent, Majesty: page one of the book now just says \"consistently\". But a canal is a canal, and the crown's collector doesn't burn the house down the year it finally builds something. Paddy, enjoy your ten a harvest. Rob, your refusal goes on the usual page.",
 "Clark: Any revolt now means Chaos, and under Chaos my set is just a scroll, a jar and a bowl. So I pay, keep the clock from running out, and wait for the step where a hundred and eighteen grain and a full set can take that chair without asking anyone.")
say("Rich", "Twenty-seven and a half, Your Majesty, and gladly. A canal on the coast, the tax steady five years running: that is a crown worth paying. I'll only note, for Clark's book, that the Gentry pays in full every year while some among us don't pay at all.",
 "Rich: Reina has just bought Paddy's vote with a canal, so the crown is strong this year and the cheapest thing I can do is pay and smile. The jab at Rob's refusal is in the record now, ready for the day I want the table thinking about who can't control the Warlord.")
say("Silke", "Fifty percent, five years running. That puts me at a hundred grain to the crown with this year's seventeen and a half, and Her Majesty is sitting on three hundred and fifty-six. Lovely canal, Paddy, truly, it'll pay you ten a year. Nothing's being dug on my coast. If anyone calls this tax what it is, I'm standing behind them, and I'll have my soldier with me.",
 "Silke: Reina dug a canal through Paddy's province to buy back the year he promised me, so now I find out whether his word costs more than forty grain of somebody else's digging. I won't call it myself and sound shrill again, but if he calls it, or Rob does, I'm the second voice, and Reina can bid for me this time instead of for Rich.")
say("Paddy", "Seventeen and a half, and I'll hand it over without the face I usually make. I said if nothing got built south of Silke's beach I'd call the cause, and something has. A canal's the sort of promise you can stand in up to your knees, so I'll call it kept. Rich, the rent's paid and the goodwill's started. Don't spend it all at once.",
 "Paddy: The canal is worth ten a harvest to me for good, and I set that deadline in public, so it'd be a poor man who pulled the roof down the year they finally fixed the gutter. I'm sitting on a hundred grain now and I've still no Dragon Jar, so I'm watching Rich. His jar is the only one I'd trust, and I've seen what his price looks like.")
say("Rob", "Five, Clark? I'll keep it, thank you, same as every year. And Your Majesty's quite right, you could set a calendar by it: half of everything, five years running, through a drought, with the locusts eating better than Silke does. If that isn't 'consistently excessive' I don't know what the words are for. I'm calling it. Paddy, I know you've just been handed a very nice ditch, but one canal doesn't make fifty percent any smaller. Silke, you know where to sign.",
 "Rob: Paddy's been bought with a canal and Rich gets bought every spring, so this one hangs on Silke and on whether anyone else is sick of paying half. Even if it falls over, everyone at the table hears the Warlord saying out loud what they're all thinking while they count their change, and my lad on M15 is still one step from Rich's Bowl.")
act("Clark, Rich and Paddy will pay. Rob refuses his 5 and calls a revolt on the cause 'taxes are consistently excessive', and Silke backs it. A revolt needs its caller and two others, so one more is needed. The answers crossed, so Clark, Rich and Paddy are each asked once whether they agree.", map_hl=[2,3,5,6,8])
say("Clark", "Rob, the cause is in my book, five years of it, dated and underlined twice. Written down is one thing, though, and called by a man who won't pay his five is another. The collector doesn't sign a revolt for a taxpayer who never pays. That's a no from me, and it goes on your page under \"cheek, again\".",
 "Clark: Chaos freezes my set, and then the crown goes to whoever has the most grain and the loudest friends, which means Reina's three hundred or Rich's purse. I'm not handing that over when I can take the chair myself, set in hand, on my own schedule.")
say("Rich", "Your Majesty, it pains me, but five years at half of everything is \"consistently\", and Clark has the book to prove it. So the Gentry agrees the cause exists. My price is posted, the same way as always: hand back my twenty-seven and a half and ten more for the trouble, and I take it back the moment it's paid, every grain, as I have twice before.",
 "Rich: Agreeing is what makes this a called revolt, and nothing stops me withdrawing before it stands, so I'm out very little and Reina has three hundred and fifty-six to calm me down with. The third time I'm paid to stand still will cost her the same as the first two: a fair price, and a promise she knows I keep.")
say("Paddy", "Rob, I gave Her Majesty a year, out loud. She spent it digging a canal through my fields, and I'm not going to stand in the wet and say she did nothing. Fifty's a heavy load, Silke, I know it, and I'll say so the day the building stops. But I won't help Rob pull the roof down the same week they've fixed the gutter.",
 "Paddy: I set the terms myself, the crown met them, and a man who moves his own fence posts after the fact won't be trusted with a gate again. Rob still wants the throne, and I'd sooner keep the steady hand that has finally fed Province 6 than hand the keys to the fellow trying the lock.")
revolt_called("Rob calls a revolt before the tax is collected, on the cause 'taxes are consistently excessive'. Silke and Rich agree; Clark and Paddy don't. With two agreeing, the revolt is called, and play stops until it stands or is dropped. Reina may answer it. (A rule worth stating: if it stood, the throne would go only by acclamation, and only claimants are counted, on their own grain in hand, the soldiers they control and their friends. Reina, deposed, couldn't claim, and the sealed reserve would pass whole to whoever was acclaimed. A claimant needs to lead two of the three counts, so Rob, with one soldier and no friends yet, would need backers to be acclaimed.)")
say("Reina", "The rate stands at fifty. I won't haggle over it in front of a man who's never paid a grain of it. Rich, here's your price, counted out in front of everyone so nobody can say the Gentry's loyalty comes cheap. And Silke, so you know I heard you: next year the crown digs the first canal section through Province 5, at the crown's own cost. The coast gets water on both sides.",
 "Reina: That's the third time I've bought Rich, and now everyone knows what he costs, which is useful too. The canal on Silke's side is the real answer: it's built once, and it doesn't need buying again every spring.")
act("Reina answers the revolt. She pays Rich 37.5 from the reserve, his posted price (his 27.5 of tax back and 10 more), and promises the first section of the Grand Canal in Silke's Province 5 next year, all 40 from the crown. The tax stays at 50%.", deltas={pid(1):-37.5, pid(3):37.5}, map_hl=[1,3,5], deal=True)
say("Rob", "I'm standing by it. Rich, that's three times now, and you've earned thirty-seven and a half for holding a pen. Next spring I'll call it again just to see what your price has gone up to. Silke, a canal promised for next year is still just a promise, but you have the coast to think of, so do what you have to. Majesty, I'll say this much: you've a very good accountant in Rich.",
 "Rob: Rich will take it back the moment the grain lands, so this one dies on the table. That's alright, though, because the whole table just watched the crown pay thirty-seven and a half to keep a landlord loyal, and my lad on M15 is still one step from that landlord's Bowl.")
say("Silke", "A canal through Province 5 next year, at the crown's cost, said out loud in front of six people. Majesty, I'll take that over Rob's empty chair, and I'm taking it back. But Rich just got thirty-seven and a half for the third time and I got a promise, so I'll be counting shovels. If there's no water on my coast by next harvest, the next call is mine, and I won't be calling it alone.",
 "Silke: Rich was bought the moment Reina counted out his grain, so the revolt was dead whether I stood or not, and the promise Paddy told me to get is now on the record. If that canal isn't dug next year, I call it with Paddy, and Reina can't say she wasn't warned.")
say("Rich", "Thirty-seven and a half, counted out where everyone could see it. The Gentry's price was posted, paid and honoured, so I take it back, every grain. And Silke, a canal on your coast next year is a better answer than any revolt would have given you.",
 "Rich: That's three times I've been paid to stand still, and the price only holds because I always keep the bargain, so I keep it now. The trouble is Rob still has a soldier one round from my Bowl and now has a grudge, so a friend between him and my pieces is next on my list.")
revolt_dropped("Silke and Rich take the revolt back after Reina's answer; Rob stands by it alone. The revolt is dropped, and play resumes.")
taxes(.50, skip=(8,), collector="Clark, the Official, collects the tax for the crown")
build("Grand Canal", 40, {1:40}, canal={6:1}, text="Public works: Reina digs the first section of the Grand Canal in Paddy's Province 6, at the crown's cost of 40. It adds 10 to Paddy's harvest from next year.", hover="Reina pays the 40, into the Harvest Pile. The Winter Palace, which the coastal canals need first, already stands.")
say("Rob", "Rich, you've been paid three times this year for being loyal, so I'm sure you won't miss a bowl. It's on M15 now, in my desert, if you'd like to come and look at it. And because I'm a generous man I've hired a lad on N12 to keep an eye on your Dragon Jar for you. Nobody at this table told me to do any of that, Majesty. I did it all myself.",
 "Rob: That's the first piece I've raided all game, and it's Rich's, which is only fair after he sold my revolt three times. The new lad on N12 is one step from his Jar at M12, so next year the Gentry has to pay for a guard or watch the whole set walk into the desert.")
march({"W1":"L14"}, take={"W1":("WB","M15")}, text="Rob marches: W1 steps onto L14 in Rich's Province 3 and takes his Wooden Bowl instead of the 5, setting it on M15 in the desert. It is the Warlord's first piece, and Rich's set is broken: he holds the Customs House and the Dragon Jar and needs a Wooden Bowl again.")
hire_soldier("N12", "Rob hires a second soldier for 20, paid to the Harvest Pile, and stands it on N12 in his desert, beside Rich's Dragon Jar on M12.")
salaries({2:15}, text="Salaries: Reina pays Clark 15 as the Official.")
